{"title":"Introduction","markdown":{"headingText":"Introduction","containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n## What the applications have in common\n\nAn OpenPFC application is a small amount of physics on top of a large amount\nof shared machinery. The machinery is a periodic structured grid decomposed\nacross MPI ranks, a distributed field type that is host- or device-resident,\nan FFT or finite-difference stack, and a session that assembles them from a\nJSON input. The physics is typically one header.\n\nFor the spectral applications that header supplies three things:\n\n* a **linear symbol** $L(k)$ — everything in the equation that is linear in\n  the unknown, expressed as a multiplier on each Fourier mode;\n* a **pointwise nonlinearity** $N(\\psi)$ — evaluated in real space, one cell\n  at a time, with no knowledge of the grid or the decomposition;\n* a **nonlinear coupling** $M_{\\mathrm{nl}}(k)$ — how the transformed\n  nonlinearity re-enters the equation.\n\nNothing else. No loops over $k$, no MPI, no hand-written kernels, and no\nseparate GPU implementation of the physics: the same header is instantiated\nfor the host and device memory spaces.\n\n## Why that shape is the interesting part\n\nThe consequence is that the derivative order of a model is not a measure of\nhow hard it is to implement. Chapter @sec-surface-diffusion solves\n$\\partial_t h = -B\\nabla^4 h$ and chapter @sec-ehd-film solves a sixth-order\nelastohydrodynamic problem; in the source the difference is that one function\nreturns $-Bk_{\\mathrm{lap}}^2$ and the other returns a cubic in\n$k_{\\mathrm{lap}}$. Both are pointwise multiplies on a transformed field.\n\nWriting the same two operators as real-space stencils would not be comparable\nin effort, and the sixth-order stencil would be wide, awkward at the\ndecomposition boundary, and expensive in halo traffic.\n\nWhat high order does cost is **stiffness**. The most negative eigenvalue of a\n$k^6$ operator on a grid of spacing $\\Delta x$ scales as $\\Delta x^{-6}$, so an\nexplicit integrator's stable timestep collapses. The applications in this\nreport deal with that by integrating the linear part exactly; chapter\n@sec-methods sets out how.\n\n## Why not spectral everywhere\n\nSpectral methods buy exactness and simplicity when the problem is periodic and\nsmooth. When it is not, they stop being the right tool. Non-periodic\nboundaries, sharp interfaces, local transport, and directional-solidification\ngeometry are all better served by high-order finite differences, and OpenPFC\nsupports both under one runtime.\n\nThe finite-difference applications in this report exist to make that concrete.\n`heat3d` in particular ships both discretisations of the *same* PDE, which is\nwhat makes the comparison in chapter @sec-scalability meaningful: it measures\nthe methods, not two different problems.\n\n## What this report contains\n\n| Part | Chapters | Character |\n|---|---|---|\n| Phase-field crystal | @sec-tungsten, @sec-aluminum | Production PFC, mean-field ETD, moving frames |\n| High-order spectral | @sec-cahn-hilliard – @sec-gradient-elasticity | $k^4$ to $k^{10}$ operators, each with an analytical check |\n| Finite difference | @sec-heat3d – @sec-kobayashi | Stencils, coupled fields, mixed boundaries |\n| Scalability | @sec-scalability | Measured LUMI-G curves, 1–32 GCDs |\n\nEvery chapter ends with a *Verification* section listing what the test suite\nactually asserts. The intent is that no statement in this report has to be\ntaken on trust: it either cites a test or is marked as unmeasured.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart